<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:dlg="clr-namespace:CircuitRF.Ui.Views.Dialogs"
        xmlns:dialogs="clr-namespace:CircuitRF.Ui.Views.Dialogs"
        x:Class="CircuitRF.Ui.Views.Dialogs.SettingsView"
        Title="circuitRF Settings"
        Width="720"
        Height="540"
        MinWidth="620"
        MinHeight="440"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner"
        ShowInTaskbar="False">

    <Grid RowDefinitions="*,Auto" Margin="14">

        <!-- ── Tabs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <TabControl Grid.Row="0" Name="Tabs">

            <!-- TabControl's DEFAULT ItemsPanel is a WrapPanel, so headers that do not fit SPILL ONTO
                 A SECOND ROW — they do not clip, and nothing errors. That is exactly how four headers
                 silently became two rows in TechEditorView as its window narrowed, and adding a fifth
                 header here brings this dialog to the same edge. The fix is the one already in the
                 tree at TechEditorView.axaml: a horizontal StackPanel keeps them on one row
                 unconditionally, and the scoped TabItem style then pins the size explicitly rather
                 than inheriting the theme's Pivot-scale header metrics — because the panel alone makes
                 one row a PROMISE and the style is what makes it a FIT.

                 SCOPED to this TabControl's own Styles, never a global TabItem selector: this dialog
                 is not the only TabControl in the application.

                 BOTH HALVES ARE NECESSARY HERE, and that was MEASURED at MinWidth="620" rather than
                 assumed — a tab strip that fits at 720 and wraps at 620 is the same defect arriving
                 later. On the theme's own header metrics the five headers measure 850 px against
                 592 px of usable width, and under the default WrapPanel "Color Theme" and "Wirebonds"
                 duly land on a SECOND ROW, silently. TechEditorView's values below bring them to
                 529 px, one row, 63 px spare.

                 (Those per-header widths also say the four-tab dialog was ALREADY over the line
                 before this tab existed: 656 px against 592. It fitted at the 720 default and spilled
                 as the window narrowed, which is exactly the failure TechEditorView records — nobody
                 had looked at 620.) -->
            <TabControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal"/>
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
            </TabControl.ItemsPanel>

            <TabControl.Styles>
                <Style Selector="TabItem">
                    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13"/>
                    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,4"/>
                    <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0"/>
                    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center"/>
                </Style>
            </TabControl.Styles>

            <!-- ── General tab ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
            <TabItem Header="General">
                <ScrollViewer Margin="0,8,0,0" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
                    <StackPanel Spacing="0">

                        <!-- On Launch section -->
                        <TextBlock Text="On Launch" FontSize="11" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                   Opacity="0.55" Margin="0,0,0,6"/>
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="130,*" RowDefinitions="Auto,6,Auto,6,Auto"
                              Margin="0,0,0,20">
                            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0"
                                       Text="Action:" VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12"/>
                            <ComboBox  Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                                       Name="LaunchActionCombo" FontSize="12" MinWidth="180"
                                       HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                       SelectionChanged="OnLaunchSettingChanged"/>
                            <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0"
                                       Text="Window Layout:" VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12"/>
                            <ComboBox  Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="1"
                                       Name="WindowLayoutCombo" FontSize="12" MinWidth="180"
                                       HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                       SelectionChanged="OnLaunchSettingChanged"/>
                            <CheckBox  Grid.Row="4" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                                       Name="ShowDockersOnLaunchCheck" FontSize="12"
                                       Content="Show Dockers"
                                       IsCheckedChanged="OnShowDockersOnLaunchChanged"/>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- Copy / Export section -->
                        <TextBlock Text="Copy / Export" FontSize="11" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                   Opacity="0.55" Margin="0,0,0,6"/>
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="130,*" RowDefinitions="Auto,6,Auto">
                            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0"
                                       Text="Copy color:" VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12"/>
                            <ComboBox  Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                                       Name="CopyColorCombo" FontSize="12" MinWidth="180"
                                       HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                       SelectionChanged="OnCopyColorChanged"/>
                            <CheckBox  Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                                       Name="TransparentBgCheck"
                                       Content="Transparent background" FontSize="12"
                                       IsCheckedChanged="OnTransparentBgChanged"/>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- Design Rules — R16d's own "not mandatory, not silent". Also reachable from
                             the pre-export prompt itself, which is where it matters most; here so a
                             user whose designs are clean can still find and change it. -->
                        <TextBlock Text="Design Rules" FontSize="11" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                   Opacity="0.55" Margin="0,20,0,6"/>
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="130,*" RowDefinitions="Auto">
                            <CheckBox  Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                                       Name="CheckDrcOnExportCheck"
                                       Content="Run DRC before exporting" FontSize="12"
                                       IsCheckedChanged="OnCheckDrcOnExportChanged"
                                       ToolTip.Tip="Runs a design-rule check before writing GDSII, DXF or Gerber. A clean design exports with no interruption; violations are shown first and the export can still go ahead."/>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- Import — R-rf3-7. ON by default: an imported board whose pours arrived as
                             tens of thousands of painted scanlines is neither editable copper nor
                             meshable, and the region is what every consumer wants. The switch is here
                             for the one case the default cannot serve — comparing an import against
                             its CAM source, or chasing an import bug. -->
                        <TextBlock Text="Import" FontSize="11" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                   Opacity="0.55" Margin="0,20,0,6"/>
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="130,*" RowDefinitions="Auto">
                            <CheckBox  Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                                       Name="CoalesceRasterFillCheck"
                                       Content="Coalesce painted fill into regions" FontSize="12"
                                       IsCheckedChanged="OnCoalesceRasterFillChanged"
                                       ToolTip.Tip="Some CAM tools paint a copper pour as thousands of abutting strokes instead of one filled region. Importing Gerber or a board file turns those back into the region they paint, which is the shape that draws quickly, meshes for EM and checks against design rules. Turn it off to get the primitives exactly as they were authored — the import reports every layer it coalesces either way."/>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- Messages section -->
                        <TextBlock Text="Messages" FontSize="11" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                   Opacity="0.55" Margin="0,20,0,6"/>
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="130,*">
                            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Timestamps:"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12"/>
                            <ComboBox  Grid.Column="1" Name="MsgTimestampCombo"
                                       FontSize="12" MinWidth="180" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                       SelectionChanged="OnMsgTimestampChanged"/>
                        </Grid>

                    </StackPanel>
                </ScrollViewer>
            </TabItem>

            <!-- ── Security & Permissions tab ─────────────────────────────────
                 Everything that decides what circuitRF is allowed to RUN or to FETCH lives here,
                 rather than being spread across the tab a feature happened to arrive in. Automatic
                 updates moved out of General for that reason (owner, 2026-08-25): the checkbox that
                 governs whether the application downloads and installs code from the internet is a
                 security setting first and a convenience second, and a user auditing what this
                 binary is permitted to do should find it in one place. -->
            <TabItem Header="Security &amp; Permissions">
                <ScrollViewer Margin="0,8,0,0" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
                    <StackPanel Spacing="0">

                        <!-- The one way back from "Don't Allow" -->
                        <TextBlock Text="External PDKs" FontSize="11" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                   Opacity="0.55" Margin="0,0,0,6"/>
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="130,*" RowDefinitions="Auto,4">
                            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Text="Generated Artwork:"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12"/>
                            <StackPanel Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="10">
                                <Button Name="ForgetPCellTrustButton" Content="Ask Again…" FontSize="12"
                                        HorizontalContentAlignment="Center"
                                        Click="OnForgetPCellTrustClick"/>
                                <TextBlock Name="PCellTrustStatus" FontSize="11" Opacity="0.6"
                                           VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                            </StackPanel>
                            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" FontSize="11" Opacity="0.6"
                                       TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,4,0,0"
                                       Text="Forgets which kits are allowed to run their own artwork scripts, so circuitRF asks about each one again."/>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- External device workers. The OTHER kind of program a kit can make
                             circuitRF run: pcell-generators.json ships scripts, device-provider.json
                             ships a worker, and until 2026-08-25 only the first of the two asked.
                             Same one-control-two-hosts arrangement as the Updates section below. -->
                        <dlg:ExternalWorkerSettingsView Margin="0,24,0,0"/>

                        <!-- Updates. The controls and their handlers live in UpdateSettingsView so
                             that harmonicaRF's own dialog — which does not use SettingsView at all —
                             hosts the SAME implementation rather than a second copy of it. Both read
                             and write the same two AppPreferences properties; neither owns them. -->
                        <dlg:UpdateSettingsView Margin="0,24,0,0"/>

                        <!-- The Verilog-A compiler, LAST on this tab (owner, 2026-09-03). It belongs
                             here at all because it names a program circuitRF is permitted to RUN,
                             which is the question this tab answers — but it is the one control here
                             that most users never touch (blank means "use PATH"), so it sits below
                             the two that govern what runs without being asked. -->
                        <dlg:VerilogACompilerSettingsView Margin="0,24,0,0"/>

                    </StackPanel>
                </ScrollViewer>
            </TabItem>

            <!-- ── Revision Control tab ───────────────────────────────────────
                 Immediately to the right of Security & Permissions (owner, 2026-09-06), and a tab of
                 its own rather than a section on that one. Security & Permissions answers exactly one
                 question — what circuitRF is allowed to RUN and to FETCH — and its own header records
                 that things were collected there for that SHAPE rather than for the tab they arrived
                 in. Naming a git executable is the only row here that would fit it; the other nine are
                 about what is KEPT, and a designer looking for "how long are my restore points kept"
                 will not look under Security.

                 SHOWN ON EVERY MACHINE, whether or not there is a git (owner, 2026-09-07). §4.3 as
                 first written hid the whole tab when git was absent, so that absence stayed silent —
                 and hiding it also hid the fact that circuitRF CAN keep a history and is not keeping
                 one here, which is the same false belief the design is written against reached from
                 the other side. Without a git the rows are greyed and one sentence says why; the git
                 path and its Detect stay live, because they are the remedy. See
                 RevisionTabAvailability. -->
            <TabItem Header="Revision Control" Name="RevisionTab">
                <ScrollViewer Margin="0,8,0,0" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
                    <dlg:RevisionControlSettingsView Name="RevisionSettings"/>
                </ScrollViewer>
            </TabItem>

            <!-- ── Color Theme tab ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
            <TabItem Header="Color Theme">
                <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*" Margin="0,8,0,0">

                    <!-- Theme selector row -->
                    <DockPanel Grid.Row="0" Margin="0,0,0,10">
                        <!-- Variant toggle (right-docked first so it doesn't collapse) -->
                        <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Right" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                            <RadioButton Name="LightRadio" Content="Light"
                                         GroupName="Variant" Click="OnVariantChanged" FontSize="12"/>
                            <RadioButton Name="DarkRadio"  Content="Dark"
                                         GroupName="Variant" Click="OnVariantChanged" FontSize="12"/>
                        </StackPanel>

                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                            <TextBlock Text="Color Theme:" VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12"/>
                            <ComboBox Name="ThemeCombo" FontSize="12" MinWidth="140"
                                      SelectionChanged="OnThemeComboChanged"/>
                            <Button Name="SaveThemeButton" Content="Save Theme…" FontSize="12"
                                    Click="OnSaveThemeClick" Padding="8,4"/>
                        </StackPanel>
                    </DockPanel>

                    <!-- Main editor panel -->
                    <Grid Grid.Row="1" ColumnDefinitions="200,10,*">

                        <!-- Role list -->
                        <Border Grid.Column="0"
                                BorderBrush="{DynamicResource ThemeBorderMidBrush}"
                                BorderThickness="1"
                                CornerRadius="4"
                                ClipToBounds="True">
                            <ListBox Name="RoleList"
                                     SelectionMode="Single"
                                     SelectionChanged="OnRoleSelected"
                                     DoubleTapped="OnRoleDoubleTapped"
                                     FontSize="11.5"
                                     Padding="2">
                                <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                                    <DataTemplate x:DataType="dialogs:RoleRowModel">
                                        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="7" Margin="2,1">
                                            <Rectangle Width="14" Height="14" RadiusX="2" RadiusY="2"
                                                       VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                                <Rectangle.Fill>
                                                    <SolidColorBrush Color="{Binding SwatchColor}"/>
                                                </Rectangle.Fill>
                                            </Rectangle>
                                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Label}" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                        </StackPanel>
                                    </DataTemplate>
                                </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                            </ListBox>
                        </Border>

                        <!-- Color editor (right panel) -->
                        <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Spacing="10">

                            <!-- Color preview swatch -->
                            <Border CornerRadius="4" ClipToBounds="True" Height="40">
                                <Rectangle Name="ColorPreviewRect"/>
                            </Border>

                            <!-- RGBA sliders + linked integer edit boxes -->
                            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="14,*,46" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto" RowSpacing="3">

                                <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Text="R" VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12" FontWeight="Medium"/>
                                <Slider    Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" Name="SliderR" Minimum="0" Maximum="255"
                                           IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                                           ValueChanged="OnSliderChanged" Margin="6,0"/>
                                <TextBox   Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2" Name="LabelR"
                                           FontSize="12" Width="42" MaxLength="3" TextAlignment="Center"
                                           HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                           FontFamily="Courier New,Consolas,monospace"
                                           LostFocus="OnRgbaBoxLostFocus" KeyDown="OnRgbaBoxKeyDown"/>

                                <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Text="G" VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12" FontWeight="Medium"/>
                                <Slider    Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" Name="SliderG" Minimum="0" Maximum="255"
                                           IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                                           ValueChanged="OnSliderChanged" Margin="6,0"/>
                                <TextBox   Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2" Name="LabelG"
                                           FontSize="12" Width="42" MaxLength="3" TextAlignment="Center"
                                           HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                           FontFamily="Courier New,Consolas,monospace"
                                           LostFocus="OnRgbaBoxLostFocus" KeyDown="OnRgbaBoxKeyDown"/>

                                <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" Text="B" VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12" FontWeight="Medium"/>
                                <Slider    Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="1" Name="SliderB" Minimum="0" Maximum="255"
                                           IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                                           ValueChanged="OnSliderChanged" Margin="6,0"/>
                                <TextBox   Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="2" Name="LabelB"
                                           FontSize="12" Width="42" MaxLength="3" TextAlignment="Center"
                                           HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                           FontFamily="Courier New,Consolas,monospace"
                                           LostFocus="OnRgbaBoxLostFocus" KeyDown="OnRgbaBoxKeyDown"/>

                                <TextBlock Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="0" Text="A" VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12" FontWeight="Medium"/>
                                <Slider    Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="1" Name="SliderA" Minimum="0" Maximum="255"
                                           IsSnapToTickEnabled="True" TickFrequency="1"
                                           ValueChanged="OnSliderChanged" Margin="6,0"/>
                                <TextBox   Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="2" Name="LabelA"
                                           FontSize="12" Width="42" MaxLength="3" TextAlignment="Center"
                                           HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                                           FontFamily="Courier New,Consolas,monospace"
                                           LostFocus="OnRgbaBoxLostFocus" KeyDown="OnRgbaBoxKeyDown"/>

                            </Grid>

                            <!-- Hex input -->
                            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
                                <TextBlock Text="#" VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12" FontWeight="Medium" Width="14"/>
                                <TextBox Name="HexBox" FontSize="12" Width="88" MaxLength="8"
                                         LostFocus="OnHexLostFocus" KeyDown="OnHexKeyDown"
                                         FontFamily="Courier New,Consolas,monospace"/>
                            </StackPanel>

                            <!-- Role path label -->
                            <TextBlock Name="RoleNameLabel" FontSize="10.5" Opacity="0.5"/>

                        </StackPanel>
                    </Grid>

                </Grid>
            </TabItem>

            <!-- ── Wirebonds tab ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->
            <TabItem Header="Wirebonds">
                <ScrollViewer Margin="0,8,0,0" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
                    <StackPanel Spacing="0">

                        <!-- wbond.md §6.4's creation defaults. Per USER, not per design: they
                             describe how one shop's bonder is set up, and a .wBond arriving from
                             someone else must not change what the next wire you draw looks like. -->
                        <TextBlock Text="Wirebonds" FontSize="11" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                   Opacity="0.55" Margin="0,0,0,6"/>
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="130,*" RowDefinitions="Auto,6,Auto,6,Auto,6,Auto,6,Auto,Auto">
                            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Text="Points per wire:"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12"/>
                            <NumericUpDown Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" Name="WBondPointsUpDown"
                                           Minimum="3" Maximum="101" Increment="1" FormatString="0"
                                           Width="110" HorizontalAlignment="Left" FontSize="12"
                                           ValueChanged="OnWBondPointsChanged"/>

                            <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" Text="Wire diameter:"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12"/>
                            <StackPanel Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
                                <NumericUpDown Name="WBondDiameterUpDown" Minimum="0.05" Maximum="20"
                                               Increment="0.1" FormatString="0.###"
                                               Width="110" FontSize="12"
                                               ValueChanged="OnWBondDiameterChanged"/>
                                <TextBlock Text="mil" VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12" Opacity="0.7"/>
                            </StackPanel>

                            <TextBlock Grid.Row="4" Grid.Column="0" Text="Wire material:"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12"/>
                            <ComboBox Grid.Row="4" Grid.Column="1" Name="WBondMaterialCombo"
                                      Width="150" HorizontalAlignment="Left" FontSize="12"
                                      SelectionChanged="OnWBondMaterialChanged"/>

                            <!-- Wire z-height (owner, 2026-08-17). Minimum is deliberately NEGATIVE and
                                 zero is a real value: a foot at z=0 lands on the reference plane and a
                                 negative one sits in a cavity below it. Both are geometry someone bonds,
                                 which is why this preference alone treats "absent" and "zero" as
                                 different things. -->
                            <TextBlock Grid.Row="6" Grid.Column="0" Text="Wire z-height:"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12"/>
                            <StackPanel Grid.Row="6" Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
                                <NumericUpDown Name="WBondFootZUpDown" Minimum="-100" Maximum="1000"
                                               Increment="1" FormatString="0.###"
                                               Width="110" FontSize="12"
                                               ValueChanged="OnWBondFootZChanged"/>
                                <TextBlock Text="mil" VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12" Opacity="0.7"/>
                            </StackPanel>

                            <TextBlock Grid.Row="8" Grid.Column="0" Text="Paste pitch:"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12"/>
                            <StackPanel Grid.Row="8" Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
                                <NumericUpDown Name="WBondPastePitchUpDown" Minimum="0.1" Maximum="1000"
                                               Increment="1" FormatString="0.###"
                                               Width="110" FontSize="12"
                                               ValueChanged="OnWBondPastePitchChanged"/>
                                <TextBlock Text="mil" VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12" Opacity="0.7"/>
                            </StackPanel>

                            <TextBlock Grid.Row="9" Grid.Column="1" FontSize="11" Opacity="0.6"
                                       TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,6,0,0"
                                       Text="Applies to newly drawn wires. Existing wires keep whatever they were drawn with. Nudge steps stay 1 mil / 5 mil — those are bonder-process quantities, not display settings.&#10;Wire z-height is where BOTH feet of a new wire land — a wire drawn in the layout view, and the wire a new wBond component is created with. The profile view is unaffected: there you click the height you want.&#10;Paste pitch places a pasted wire clear of what is already there, stepping in +y until it lands free. It never re-spaces the wires on the clipboard."/>
                        </Grid>

                        <!-- The built-in assembly rule set's one number. It lives on the WIREBONDS tab
                             rather than beside "Run DRC before exporting" because what a
                             user is looking for here is a wire dimension, and it sits next to the
                             other four. -->
                        <TextBlock Text="Design Rules" FontSize="11" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                   Opacity="0.55" Margin="0,20,0,6"/>
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="130,*" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto">
                            <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Text="Wire clearance:"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12"/>
                            <StackPanel Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="6">
                                <NumericUpDown Name="WireClearanceUpDown" Minimum="0" Maximum="100"
                                               Increment="0.1" FormatString="0.###"
                                               Width="110" FontSize="12"
                                               ValueChanged="OnWireClearanceChanged"/>
                                <TextBlock Text="mil" VerticalAlignment="Center" FontSize="12" Opacity="0.7"/>
                            </StackPanel>
                            <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" FontSize="11" Opacity="0.6"
                                       TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,6,0,0"
                                       Text="circuitRF's built-in assembly rule: how close two wires may pass, measured surface to surface between their outer edges. Reported as an error by a design-rule check, whether or not a .wasm assembly rule file is referenced — a house's rule file is not what makes overlapping metal invalid. Zero reports only wires that actually touch or overlap. A .wasm's own spacing rules are checked as well as this one, never instead of it."/>
                        </Grid>

                    </StackPanel>
                </ScrollViewer>
            </TabItem>

        </TabControl>

        <!-- ── Footer buttons (shared across all tabs) ─────────────────────
             Help at the leading edge and everything that ACTS on the dialog at the trailing one,
             which is the platform convention on all three operating systems. Revert moved across
             to join them (owner, 2026-09-03): it is a destructive action on the settings, and the
             bottom-left corner is where a reader looks for the documentation, not for a button
             that throws their edits away. -->
        <DockPanel Grid.Row="1" Margin="0,10,0,0">
            <Button DockPanel.Dock="Left" Name="HelpButton" Content="Help" FontSize="12"
                    Click="OnHelpClick" Padding="8,4"
                    ToolTip.Tip="Open the Settings chapter of the circuitRF User Documentation"/>
            <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Right" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8" HorizontalAlignment="Right">
                <Button Content="Revert" FontSize="12" Padding="8,4"
                        HorizontalContentAlignment="Center" Click="OnRevertClick"/>
                <Button Content="Cancel" FontSize="12" Width="80"
                        HorizontalContentAlignment="Center" Click="OnCancelClick"/>
                <Button Content="Close"  FontSize="12" Width="80" IsDefault="True"
                        Classes="accent" HorizontalContentAlignment="Center" Click="OnCloseClick"/>
            </StackPanel>
        </DockPanel>

    </Grid>
</Window>
